  <RecipeSchema {...recipe} datePublished="2026-07-26" dateModified="2026-07-26" url={canonical} />

  <h1>Salmon Jerky</h1>

  <div class="answer-box">
    <p>Salmon jerky is fresh salmon sliced thin, brined, and slowly dried into a chewy, protein- and omega-3-rich snack. Unlike beef, salmon needs a food-safety <strong>freeze step</strong> first (FDA: −4°F for 7 days) and dries at a lower <strong>145°F</strong>. You can make it in a dehydrator, smoker, or oven — smoked and candied are the most popular styles.</p>
  </div>

  <ContentImage src="/images/salmon-jerky.webp" alt="Homemade salmon jerky strips, glossy and deep orange, stacked on a board" eager={true} />

  <p>If you searched for the <strong>best salmon jerky</strong>, you’ll find it’s expensive to buy and easy to make for a fraction of the price. This guide covers <strong>salmon jerky for humans</strong> — the real snack, as opposed to the dog treats sold under the same name (there’s a safe dog version further down). Salmon jerky is simply salmon, cured in a light brine and dried until chewy; done right it tastes like a cross between smoked salmon and jerky, not "fishy" at all.</p>

  <h2>Salmon Jerky Recipe</h2>
  <p>This is the base <strong>salmon jerky recipe</strong>, and it’s the answer to how to make salmon jerky in any appliance — dehydrator, smoker, or oven. The method is the same three moves as meat jerky (slice, brine, dry) with one fish-specific safety step added up front. Because salmon is delicate and oily, it dries faster and at a lower temperature than beef.</p>

  <div class="tips-block">
    <p><strong>⚠️ Fish safety — do this before anything else.</strong> Salmon is not beef, and the safety rules are different. Raw salmon can carry parasites, so unless your fish is labeled sushi-grade or previously frozen, <strong>freeze it at −4°F (−20°C) for 7 days first</strong> — the <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/selecting-and-serving-fresh-and-frozen-seafood-safely">FDA</a> parasite-destruction guideline, and the single step most recipes skip. Fish is safely cooked at an internal <strong>145°F</strong> per the <a href="https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart">USDA</a> — do <em>not</em> use the 160°F beef or 165°F poultry rule here.</p>
  </div>

  <RecipeCardBlock
    title={recipe.name}
    image={recipe.image}
    description={recipe.description}
    prepTime={recipe.prepTime}
    cookTime={recipe.cookTime}
